Cloud Networking & Infrastructure Implementation Guide
1. Project Overview & Objectives
This project demonstrates foundational networking and cloud infrastructure capabilities by deploying a web server on AWS and configuring custom DNS routing.

Core Competencies Demonstrated:
Network Fundamentals: Practical application of IP addressing, DNS record management, port configurations, and traffic routing.
Cloud Infrastructure: Provisioning and configuring Amazon EC2 compute instances within AWS.
Web Hosting & Troubleshooting: Setting up NGINX web servers and verifying end-to-end HTTP connectivity.
2. Technical Requirements & Architecture
Domain Registration: Custom domain procured via Cloudflare or AWS Route53.
Compute: AWS EC2 instance running Linux (Amazon Linux 2 / Ubuntu).
Networking / Security: Security groups configured with inbound rules permitting HTTP traffic on Port 80.
DNS Configuration: Custom A Record mapping the domain to the EC2 instance's public IPv4 address.
3. Implementation Steps
Step 1: Compute Provisioning (AWS EC2)
Launched an EC2 instance using a preferred Linux distribution.
Configured security groups to allow public HTTP web traffic:
Protocol: TCP
Port Range: 80
Source: 0.0.0.0/0 (Anywhere)
Step 2: Web Server Deployment (NGINX)
Installed and configured the NGINX web server to handle incoming HTTP requests.
Example execution for Amazon Linux:

Bash
sudo yum install -y nginx
sudo systemctl enable nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx
Step 3: DNS Configuration & Routing
Configured DNS management via Cloudflare or AWS Route53.
Created an A Record pointing the custom domain to the EC2 public IPv4 address (e.g., mapping yourdomain.co.uk to the instance IP).
Step 4: Validation & Testing
Verified DNS propagation and tested HTTP resolution by navigating to the custom domain in a web browser.
Confirmed successful rendering of the default NGINX landing page.
